l reporter who never responded. NAILS Magazine has taught this DIY route for over a decade because it works, sometimes, for the salon owner with the time to cold-pitch journalists for weeks with no guarantee anyone answers. You are past that now. What you are actually weighing is whether a done-for-you **editorial feature for salons** is worth paying for, and whether it is legitimate.\n\nThat is a reasonable thing to be unsure about. Search \"get featured\" as a paid service and you will find operations like Feature Spotlight, selling Apple News \"Top Professional\" placements starting around $197, using the exact scarcity language that should make you suspicious: \"you've been chosen,\" \"limited spots.\" They serve realtors, healthcare, tech, and real estate. Zero nail or beauty specialization. That model proves paid features work commercially, and it also proves nobody has built one for your industry specifically. That gap is exactly where Mirellé Inspo sits.\n\n### What Mirellé Inspo Actually Does\n\nMirellé Inspo publishes editorial features on nail salons and nail techs, written by an editor, reviewed by you before it goes live, and backed by an existing nail-industry readership rather than a generic syndication network. This is **press coverage for nail salons**, built by people who work inside the nail industry, not adapted from a template built for lawyers.\n\nHere is how to get my nail salon featured, in three steps. First, you tell Mirellé Inspo about your salon or your work, what makes it worth writing about, and what you want a reader to walk away knowing. Second, an editor writes the piece, the way a trade publication would, not the way a sponsored listing reads. Third, you review it before it publishes and flag anything that does not represent your business fairly. Once it is live, it stays live, with a permanent link back to your site or booking page.\n\nThat last part matters more than it sounds like it should. Local media coverage and backlinks fro